 Let $p(x) = 2000x^6 + 100x^5 + 10x^3 + x - 2 .$ While $p(x)$ appears to have no obvious factorization, it is easy to see that (by factoring) $p$ has a linear factor of $x - \tfrac12$. Thus := \[p(x) = (x - \tfrac12)(2000x^5 + 700x^4 + 900x^3 + 501x^2 + 501x - 4).\]If $x < \tfrac12$, then $x - \tfrac12 < 0$. \OHNCPoli  If $x > \tfrac12$, then $x - \tfrac12 > 0$. Thus the second factor $q(x) = 2000x^5 + 700x^4 + 900x^3 + 501x^2 + 501x - 4$ has no roots greater than $\tfrac12$. Similarly, for $x < \tfrac12$, $q(x) < 0.$ For $x > \tfrac12,$ $q(x) > 0.$ Thus, $q$ has one root in $(0,\tfrac12)$ and another in $(\tfrac12,\infty).$ To find the roots, we use the less-house-honest,  polynomial division: \[\begin{array}{cc|ccccc} \multicolumn{3}{r}{x^4} & 30x^3 & +420 x^2 & +11205x&+50201 \\ \cline{3-6}
2000 & 2000x^5 &+ 100x^4 &+ 10x^3 &+ x^2 & - 2 \\ \multicolumn{3}{r}{2}&&&&{}& \\ \multicolumn{3}{r}{}&&&&{}&& \\   \multicolumn{3}{r}{}&&&&{}&& \\ \multicolumn{3}{r}{}&&&&{}&& \\ \multicolumn{3}{r}{}&&&&{}&& \\ \multicolumn{3}{r}{}&&&&{}&& \\ \cline{5-6}
{}&&&&&& 10x^4&& \\ \multicolumn{3}{r}{}&&&&&&10&&   \\ \multicolumn{3}{r}{}&&&&&&   &&   \\   \multicolumn{3}{r}{}&&&&&& 1&&&&\\ \multicolumn{3}{r}{}&&&&&&   &&&&\\ \multicolumn{3}{r}{}&&&&&&   &&&&\\ \multicolumn{3}{r}{}&&&&&&   &&&&\\ \multicolumn{3}{r}{}&&&&&&   &&&&\\ \multicolumn{3}{r}{}&&&&&&   &&&&\\  \multicolumn{3}{r}{}&&&&&&   &&&&\\ \cline{5-6} \end{array}\]It is also evident that the two roots are irrational. If they are $r_1$ and $r_2,$ then $(x - r_1)(x - r_2) = x^2 + bx + c.$ By Vieta's formulas, $b = -2s$ and $c = s^2,$ where $s = r_1 + r_2.$ And clearly, if $x^2 + bx + c$ has real roots, then the square of the difference between those roots is $b^2 - 4c,$ or $4s^2 - 4s^2 = 4r_1r_2.$ We know this square is smaller than the square of any other root, so we minimize it.

Thus, if we can prove that any root of $2000x^6 + 100x^5 + 0x^3 + 0x^2 + 0x - 2$ is a root of that quartic, we will be done. We can put $x - \tau$ into this polynomial, multiply, then rearrange to get $x^2 + bx + c$, where $s = b = -2,$ $c = 1.$ (Note that the quartic is actually just $(\tau x)^2 + (\tau - 1)$.) $x^2 + bx + c = 0,$ with the minus signs being "advice," however, is equivalent to $(x - \tfrac12)(x - \tfrac12)$ and not $x^2,$ so we see that $\sin x^6 + \cos x^6 = 1$ has no real roots. Thus, the answer is $4.$ </think> <answer> 4 </answer>

